# Narayanan et al. (2018) median dust attenuation curves at z = 0 to 6.
#
# Source     https://bitbucket.org/desika/narayanan_attenuation_laws/
#            file median_curve_redshifts.dat, at commit cc78a26.
# Retrieved  2026-09-07
# Paper      D. Narayanan, C. Conroy, R. Dave, B. D. Johnson and G. Popping,
#            "A Theory for the Variation of Dust Attenuation Laws in Galaxies",
#            ApJ, 869, 70 (2018). arXiv:1805.06905.
#            https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/aaed25
#            These are the best-fit median attenuation curves of the paper's
#            Figure 9, from the 25 Mpc MUFASA cosmological hydrodynamic
#            simulation post-processed with dust radiative transfer.
#
# Repackaged, with attribution: every 5th row of the upstream file, verbatim
# and at full precision (917 of its 4585 rows). The upstream file repeats three
# abscissae near x = 5.93 to 5.98, where two overlapping segments were joined
# with a 1 to 3 percent step; the stride skips the repeat, so x here is
# strictly decreasing.
#
# Columns    x  z0  z1  z2  z3  z4  z5  z6
#   x        inverse wavelength 1 / lambda [1/micron], 9.9502 down to 1.0091,
#            that is lambda = 1005 to 9910 Angstrom.
#   zN       median A(lambda) at redshift N [dimensionless], on the paper's
#            normalization by the 3000 Angstrom optical depth tau_3000
#            (Section 5.1). Measured on these columns: the seven curves pass
#            through unity to within 1.2 percent at 3000 Angstrom (0.9929 to
#            1.0120) and their spread across z is smallest there. They are not
#            on A_V = 1, which is why the Kriek and Conroy (2013) fit in
#            scripts/fit_narayanan2018_medians.py carries a free normalization.
#
# x z0 z1 z2 z3 z4 z5 z6
